  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A slow moving front will bring the potential for \nboth damaging wind gusts and severe thunderstorms this afternoon \nthrough Saturday.\n\nA cold front currently along the Appalachian Mountains will slide \nsoutheast over the next couple of days. While scattered \nthunderstorms are expected this afternoon, coverage in thunderstorms \nwill peak on Friday, then decrease Saturday and especially into \nSunday. There is a marginal (level 1 of 5) threat for severe \nthunderstorms this afternoon, primarily northwest of I-85. The \nmarginal threat for severe thunderstorms expands across the entire \nforecast area for Friday, with the threat both days coming from \ndamaging wind gusts. In addition, there is a risk for excessive \nrainfall - a marginal risk (level 1 of 4) everywhere this afternoon, \nmarginal/slight (level 2 of 4) risk Friday (marginal west of US-1, \nslight east of US-1), and a marginal risk nearly everywhere on \nSaturday. Storm motion will be slow, and this will allow for the \npotential that showers and storms will repeatedly move over the same \nlocation. Effective wind shear will be at or below 30 kt, limiting \nthe amount of organization that will occur with the thunderstorms \nthat develop.\n\nAs of 2pm, the bulk of the showers so far today had been across the \nwestern half of the forecast area, although activity is expected to \nincrease during the afternoon in southern counties. On Friday, the \ngreatest chance for rain (~60%) should be southeast of the I-85 \ncorridor. Different ensembles are depicting an axis of heavier \nrainfall in slightly different locations Friday afternoon, likely \nsomewhere along or east of the US-1 corridor. However, both the HREF \nand REFS solutions generally fall into the area of slight risk of \nexcessive rainfall, so this is consistent.  The bulk of the activity \nshifts to the I-95 corridor for Saturday afternoon, with just an \nisolated shower/storm along I-95 Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures to start the month of \nSeptember.\n\nAloft, the trough will weaken as it shifts ewd on Sat, with the \nridge building into the region Sun through mid-week. The guidance \nvaries with the overall location and orientation of the high from \nSun onward, which could impact the heating and convection potential. \nAt the surface, a pressure trough may remain in place across the \narea through mid-week, with high pressure offshore and over the \nAppalachians. Low level thicknesses should generally be in the 1405m \nto 1420m range across the area. Temperatures should be near normal \non Sat, then moderate through mid-week, with highs in the low to mid \n90s (8-12 degrees above normal) and lows in the low to mid 70s to \nstart the month of September.",
